# What the build is not allowed to see.
#
# This file is step one of Stage 6 for a reason: it decides the build CONTEXT - everything
# the daemon copies before a single instruction runs. Two things follow from that.
#
# 1. SECRETS. `.env` in the context is one `COPY . .` away from living in a layer forever,
#    and `docker history` shows layers to anyone who pulls the image. The rule is that the
#    key never enters the build at all; it arrives at runtime through the environment.
# 2. SIZE. Without this file the context here is about 1.6 GB, of which 1.3 GB is a
#    virtualenv the image must not reuse (it holds macOS arm64 wheels) and 272 MB is the
#    PDFs the index was built from. With it, the context is roughly 12 MB.
#
# The Dockerfile still copies only what it needs - this file is about what is even
# offered. Excluding a directory here means a later `COPY` of it silently gets nothing,
# which is the one failure mode of .dockerignore worth remembering.

# Environments and caches that are architecture-specific or simply large
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Secrets and local settings. `.env.example` is deliberately NOT excluded.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.vscode/

# History and working notes
.git/
.gitignore
.learning/
scratch/

# Data: the index ships, the rest does not.
#   data/index/          7.2 MB  chunks.jsonl + vectors.npy - the only runtime data
#   data/papers/         272 MB  source PDFs; the index is the artefact, not these
#   data/chunks/         2.2 MB  ingestion intermediate
#   data/embedding_cache/ 30 MB  a runtime WRITE target, decided in step 5, not baked here
#   data/eval/           1.3 MB  past eval runs
data/papers/
data/chunks/
data/embedding_cache/
data/eval/

# Build plumbing itself
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
